      "latency_ms": 2663.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 594,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (water in rivers, oceans, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater has several unusual and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans maintain their chemical balance.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This moderates the Earth's climate and prevents coastal areas from experiencing extreme temperature swings.\n* **Expansion When Freezing:** Unlike most substances, water expands and becomes *less* dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n\n### 3. Ecological and Biological View\n* **Earth’s Lifeblood:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly recycled through evaporation (turning into vapor), condensation (forming clouds), precipitation (rain and snow), and collection (flowing back into oceans and rivers). The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n* **Fuel for Life:** The human body is made of about 60% water. It acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. Without water, human beings can only survive for about 3 to 5 days.\n\n**In short:** Water is a simple molecule made of hydrogen and oxygen, but it is also the magical elixir that makes planet Earth unique, dynamic, and habitable."
